# Test: two-tier overflow pool delivers all routes under burst
# VALIDATES: AC-10/AC-11 -- overflow dispatch preserves ordering and delivery
# VALIDATES: AC-12 -- pool exhaustion does not drop routes
# PREVENTS: routes silently dropped during overflow, pool handle leaks
#
# Flow:
# 1. Force a tiny forward-worker channel (`ZE_FWD_CHAN_SIZE=2`) so overflow is guaranteed
# 2. Source peer sends 50 UPDATEs in a burst
# 3. Destination peer asserts all 50 NLRIs arrive in order (ordered= needles:
#    the forward rail may legally pack several same-attr NLRIs into one
#    UPDATE via fwdBucketMerge, so per-message framing is not asserted)
# 4. Python observer smoke-checks metric registration while the tiny channel
#    and full destination delivery prove the overflow path was taken

# Source peer (ASN 65001) -- sends 50 prefixes in a burst
option=env:var=ze.log.bgp.reactor.session:value=debug
stdin=source:terminator=EOF_SOURCE
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=20s
# linger: hold the source session open after its script completes. Closing it
# makes ze correctly withdraw the source's routes toward dest, racing the very
# forwarding burst this test asserts (announce 0000-0006 then withdraw seen).
option=linger:value=true

# Send 100 UPDATEs: 10.0.0.0/24 through 10.0.99.0/24
# Each UPDATE: ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH=[65001], NEXT_HOP=1.1.1.1, LOCAL_PREF=100
# Base hex: header(19B marker + 2B len + 1B type=2) + body
# Withdrawn=0x0000, attrs=0x15 bytes, NLRI=0x180A00XX
# We send them as individual UPDATEs to maximize per-item dispatch pressure.
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00300200000015400101004002004003040101010140050400000064180A0000
action=send:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00300200000015400101004002004003040101010140050400000064180A0001
